+ {"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["graph","propagation","engine","spec-001"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-09-20T19:46:05.320192+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"AUG-008","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P1","reasoning":"docs/SPEC-001-graph.md section 4 BEAT 4 and section 5. This is the mechanic that closes branches, and the engine is not one without it. When a question becomes answered|linked|moot: (a) every question with a derives_from edge from it is re-examined - if its blockers are resolved and it is now answerable from existing evidence it is GATED and linked rather than asked, otherwise it becomes newly askable; (b) when a decision is invalidated by a breaks edge, every question that decision closed becomes moot with the reason recorded, and that change propagates recursively. Engineering calls already concluded in the spec: branches DO reopen automatically when the answer that closed them is invalidated, with the reason recorded, because a stale closed branch silently reads as done and is worse than an open one. Acceptance: a question whose parent decision is invalidated by a breaks edge becomes moot with a reason naming the edge; the cascade reaches grandchildren; a question with an unresolved `blocks` edge is never surfaced by ask; a question that becomes answerable after a parent closes is LINKED by the gate rather than asked.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"[P1] propagation: closure and moot cascades — SPEC-001 BEAT 4","updated_at":"2026-09-20T19:46:05.320192+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"0b2bcdc3ec4d732ad69d42e9f98eb91df465cf4a6c571aab6ca3293a4eb1544d"}}
+ {"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["gitreins","quality","infra"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-09-20T21:10:24.106713+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"GITREINS-JUDGE","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P1","reasoning":"Supervisor Phase 2E audit (2026-09-20): /home/kara/auger has NO .gitreins/ directory. Every other coding-hermes project carries an evaluator block with defaults.model=deepseek-v4-flash and defaults.api_key_env=GITREINS_LLM_API_KEY; without it the Tier-2 judge never runs and gitreins commits fall back to Tier-1 guards only. Repo is small (~7 Python files, tests/, docs/), so the default limits are adequate: max_iterations >= 100, max_time >= 10m, max_input_tokens >= 250k. Fix by running the standard gitreins install (skill gitreins-integration / gitreins-workflow) in this workdir, then verify with: ls .gitreins/config.yaml && python3 -c \"import yaml;print(yaml.safe_load(open('.gitreins/config.yaml'))['evaluator'])\" and confirm a Tier-2 verdict lands.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"Configure the GitReins LLM judge (.gitreins/config.yaml) — none exists yet, so every worker commit in this repo is unjudged","updated_at":"2026-09-20T21:10:24.106713+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"59f1e3dadcc616ecf49f4ec565ff52b228650a5c80832e7aedef29fb3959af83"}}
++{"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["bundle","schema","spec-002"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.385675+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"AUG-009","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P1","reasoning":"docs/SPEC-002-bundles.md section 1. The fleet's projects come in bundles (Bane subgoal) and the IMPACT pass currently searches ONE namespace, so a decision about a shared contract is invisible to the siblings that must honour it. Two tables plus one field: bundle(id B-01, name, description, contract, status), bundle_member(id BM-0001, bundle_id, project, role owner|consumer|test-target, note), and decision.scope (project|bundle; empty = project, which is the unchanged default). Membership is a table and not a column because a project can belong to several bundles (a harness is both an ecosystem member and its own project). Acceptance: `auger init` declares both tables idempotently; a bundle-scoped decision round-trips with its scope intact; the default for an existing decision row is project (no behaviour change on migration); a bundle_member naming a project with no scheduler row is REFUSED with a clear error.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"[P1] bundle model: bundle + bundle_member tables, and decision.scope (project|bundle) — SPEC-002 1","updated_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.385675+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"bfb9977dd60d5efb991b9a0e041662f39cb6669df48cca4217019f03d3213bd3"}}
++{"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["bundle","engine","gate","spec-002"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.391009+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"AUG-010","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P1","reasoning":"docs/SPEC-002-bundles.md sections 2.1 and 2.2. The two behaviours that make bundling worth having. (a) GATE searches EVERY member's namespace: evidence = recall_many([ns(m) for m in bundle_members(p)], q) - this is cross-project decision dedupe, so if terminal-jail already decided how a sandbox isolates the filesystem, bunker LINKS that answer instead of re-deciding it and drifting; the satisfies edge gains src_project so the reader sees it came from a sibling. (b) IMPACT crosses the boundary for bundle-scoped decisions only: a project-local decision never crosses (or every answer reaches every project and the pass is quadratic again), and a breaks edge INTO A SIBLING IS AN ESCALATION, NOT A SILENT CHANGE - the engine records the break and surfaces it, it never rewrites another project's spec. Depth triage for a bundle-scoped decision takes the MAX blast radius across members. Acceptance: a question already answered in a sibling namespace is LINKED not asked; a bundle-scoped answer that invalidates a sibling decision produces a breaks edge naming that sibling and an escalation row; a project-scoped answer produces NO cross-project edge.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"[P1] bundle-aware GATE and cross-boundary IMPACT — SPEC-002 2.1/2.2","updated_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.391009+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"283676204b1d0d22ad891821c7718a6ff3e8b02e4b5f5898fca7f2931ec525e7"}}
