++{"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["bundle","engine","gate","spec-002"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.391009+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"AUG-010","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P1","reasoning":"docs/SPEC-002-bundles.md sections 2.1 and 2.2. The two behaviours that make bundling worth having. (a) GATE searches EVERY member's namespace: evidence = recall_many([ns(m) for m in bundle_members(p)], q) - this is cross-project decision dedupe, so if terminal-jail already decided how a sandbox isolates the filesystem, bunker LINKS that answer instead of re-deciding it and drifting; the satisfies edge gains src_project so the reader sees it came from a sibling. (b) IMPACT crosses the boundary for bundle-scoped decisions only: a project-local decision never crosses (or every answer reaches every project and the pass is quadratic again), and a breaks edge INTO A SIBLING IS AN ESCALATION, NOT A SILENT CHANGE - the engine records the break and surfaces it, it never rewrites another project's spec. Depth triage for a bundle-scoped decision takes the MAX blast radius across members. Acceptance: a question already answered in a sibling namespace is LINKED not asked; a bundle-scoped answer that invalidates a sibling decision produces a breaks edge naming that sibling and an escalation row; a project-scoped answer produces NO cross-project edge.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"[P1] bundle-aware GATE and cross-boundary IMPACT — SPEC-002 2.1/2.2","updated_at":"2026-09-20T23:39:09.391009+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"283676204b1d0d22ad891821c7718a6ff3e8b02e4b5f5898fca7f2931ec525e7"}}
++{"attempts":0,"blocked_reason":null,"blocked_since":null,"blocks":[],"capability_tags":["duckbrain","http","reliability"],"ci_result":null,"commit_hash":null,"completed_at":null,"complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-09-21T00:28:47.802316+00:00","depends_on":[],"dispatched_at":null,"exit_code":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"files_changed":[],"foreman_note":null,"guard_result":null,"id":"AUG-011","lines_added":0,"lines_removed":0,"perpetual":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"priority":"P2","reasoning":"DuckBrain's HTTP API runs a token-bucket limiter (src/auth/ratelimit.ts; live limit measured at 600 requests/minute per IP, X-RateLimit-Limit: 600). auger hit it in two distinct ways during this tick: (a) the transport had no retry, so a 429 propagated to the caller and cmd_init then read the ERROR body as a namespace list and crashed ('str' object has no attribute 'get') while leaving its namespace behind; (b) a test namespace leaked because the teardown DELETE itself 429'd. (a) is fixed in auger (d2dcad3: bounded retry honouring retryAfter/Retry-After, plus init failing loudly on a non-200 list). (b) is NOT fixed: teardown_namespace does not retry, so a 429 during teardown still leaks a registry row. Separately, and this one is DuckBrain's, not auger's: DELETE /api/namespaces/<n> leaves NO record of a namespace whose directory is gone — 19 stale test entries accumulated in /api/namespaces (cleared by hand this tick) because the directory removal and the registry delete are independent. Scope: (i) auger teardown retries on 429; (ii) check whether the stale-registry class is DuckBrain-wide (it affects any tool that creates namespaces) and file it against the duckbrain board if so.","review_notes":null,"status":"pending","title":"[P2] DuckBrain's 429 backpressure is unhandled by every caller (found by AUG-006's tier-1 judge)","updated_at":"2026-09-21T00:28:47.802316+00:00","worker_status":"pending","worker_summary":null,"detail":{"fin